  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Partial fit: in-band pcid lets a full envelope carry its own interpretation hint without filenames, but the scenario asks whether CID codec identity alone distinguishes pointer objects from raw payload, and this variant does not define that.",
    "strengths": [
      "pcid travels with the envelope, so type-dispatch info is not tied to local paths.",
      "The spec draws a clear boundary between opaque payload bytes and higher-level interpretation.",
      "Unknown pcid results must be marked unsupported and unverified rather than silently accepted."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "CID codec identity alone is not specified to decide raw chunk versus pointer object.",
      "If only detached payload bytes are addressed, the distinguishing pcid context is lost.",
      "Signature semantics and durable pcid resolution are left outside the envelope spec."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Best-effort inspection of unknown pcid values could still encourage over-interpretation of unverified bytes.",
      "Long-term interoperability depends on preserving pcid-to-handler knowledge over time.",
      "Draft status means the object-type binding story may still change."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Is the content-addressed unit in this scenario the whole envelope or only payload bytes?",
      "What exact rule, if any, maps CID codec identity to pointer-versus-raw handling here?",
      "How are pcid definitions discovered and kept durable without a central registry?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
